Bounded scratch PCSX2 probes for hardware-vs-RRV subsystem localisation.
Applies after pcsx2-gt-capture.patch and pcsx2-gt-guest-trace.patch to pinned
PCSX2 d5f75c9e4. All four probes are default off and share one log file
(RRV_GT_WATCH_LOG, default stderr) and one phase gate (RRV_GT_WATCH_PHASE).

  RRV_GT_WATCH=<hexaddr>:<hexlen>[,...]  EE write-watch. Installs PCSX2's own
      memchecks from the environment and AGGREGATES the hits per writer PC
      (recMemcheck also emits the call for LOG-only checks; dynarecMemcheck
      counts and returns instead of pausing the VM). Answers "which guest PC
      writes this range, how often" on hardware. The mirror on our side is
      RRV_PATH_WATCH + RRV_PATH_WATCH_HIST.
  RRV_GT_FNHITS=<hex>[,...]              execution counter for named guest PCs.
  RRV_GT_BLOCKS=1                        counts EVERY recompiled block entry.
      Mirror: our RRV_FN_HITS=all. Diffing the two executed sets for one scene
      names the guest code one engine runs and the other does not.
  RRV_GT_MEMDUMP=<hexaddr>:<hexlen>      hex-dumps one guest range per report,
      in the same format our runtime's RRV_GT_MEMDUMP prints.
  RRV_GT_FNTRACE=<hex>[,...]             for the same PCs, log the first
      RRV_GT_FNTRACE_MAX (default 40) hits with $ra and $a0..$a3, which names
      the CALLER of a routine hardware runs and we do not.

Reports carry the scene selector [0x334E04] so dumps align to the same guest
coordinate on both engines (the demo race is sel=0x00020005), and per-interval
deltas so a frozen subsystem is visible without diffing whole logs. NOTE: a
report covers the interval BEFORE it, so an interval straddling a selector edge
mixes two scenes -- exclude those before reading a histogram.

